%pdxdoc 0.1
%tool  mkdir
%ver   1.0.0
%wave  R50
%repo  github.com/paideia-os/mkdir
%plan  design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §5.9 (paideia-os)

# NAME

mkdir — create directories under the invoker's PdxFS subtree

# SYNOPSIS

    mkdir [-p] [-v] [--dry-run] [--json] [--schema] [--help] [--version] <path> [<path>...]

`--help` is listed for discoverability only — mkdir itself never parses
it. The shell intercepts `--help` before exec and dispatches to `doc
mkdir` (this document); `parse_flags_from_argv` has no recogniser for
it and never will (see the FLAGS entry below). Every other flag shown
here IS parsed by mkdir's own `parse_flags_from_argv`.

# DESCRIPTION

`mkdir` opens one `KIND_PDXFS_TXN`, creates one or more directories under
a target parent, stamps the invoker's `KIND_USER` cap as the owner tail
of every created inode, and commits the TXN. Every operation takes place
inside the invoker's own PdxFS subtree — at v1.0 there is no
libpdx-elevate hop and no cross-subtree write path (per §5.9 "no elevate
dependency" line of the wave plan).

Under `-p` the entire multi-level path lives inside the single TXN: an
abort mid-walk leaves zero new directories on disk, and pre-existing
levels are treated as no-ops rather than errors. The undo record queued
via `libpdx-audit` (`RmdirUndoRecord[]`) only lists levels this
invocation actually created — replay is `rmdir <path>` walked
leaf-to-root over those levels alone.

The TXN is scoped to the WHOLE invocation, not to one `<path>` operand:
`mkdir a b c` opens one TXN, creates all three (recording one
`CreatedDirRecord` and one `RmdirUndoRecord` per level across every
operand), and commits once. If any operand fails, the shared TXN is
never committed, so nothing any operand staged is persisted — the same
atomicity guarantee `-p` gives one multi-level path extends to every
positional in the argv list.

The primary output is the `CreatedDirRecord[]` schema stream on the
semantic pipe (cap slot 3, `KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT` bound at spawn by the
shell to a `libpdx-semantic-pipe` writer). Per I4 in
`design/tooling/plan.md`, the text layer on stderr is diagnostic only —
`-v` and every error emit one line.

# FLAGS

    -p                  Create parent directories as needed. Multi-level
                        path atomic within one TXN. Pre-existing levels
                        are no-ops (not errors). Undo only unwinds
                        levels this invocation actually created.

    -v                  Verbose. Emit one `mkdir: created <path>\n`
                        stderr line per created level. Under `-p` this
                        fires per created level, not per invocation.

    --dry-run           Parse + split-path + emit the CreatedDirRecord
                        text render, but skip Steps 6b (create) and 7
                        (TXN commit). No side effects. Exit 0.

    --json              NOT YET IMPLEMENTED (tracked as mkdir.ENH-002,
                        milestone #6). Design intent: emit the
                        CreatedDirRecord[] stream as JSON on stdout,
                        suppressing the semantic-pipe frame — I3
                        cross-tool contract. `parse_flags_from_argv`
                        has no recogniser for `json` yet; passing
                        `--json` today is silently ignored (like any
                        other unrecognised flag at v1.0).

    --schema            Print CreatedDirRecord@0.1 schema definition and
                        exit 0. I3 cross-tool contract.

    --help              NOT parsed by mkdir. The shell intercepts
                        --help before exec and dispatches to
                        `doc mkdir`, which renders this document —
                        mkdir's own parse_flags_from_argv has no
                        recogniser for --help and is not meant to grow
                        one (14 tools should not each inline a help
                        renderer).

    --version           Print `mkdir 1.0.0 (build unknown) sig=placeholder`
                        and exit 0. `build`/`sig` are static placeholders
                        (mkdir.ENH-003) — there is no build-time hash or
                        signature substitution mechanism in this tree;
                        the real values arrive when the T-INFRA-002
                        signing-bot pass wires one up.

# EXIT CODES

    0   MK_OK                        success
    1   MK_ARGV_ERR                  libpdx-argv reported non-zero
    2   MK_MISSING_PATH              no positional path argument
    3   MK_MULTI_LEVEL_UNSUPPORTED   path has '/' and -p was NOT passed
    4   MK_TXN_OPEN_FAIL             SLOT_TXN cap-invoke failed
    5   MK_MKDIR_FAIL                SLOT_PARENT_FILE or PXT_OP_CREATE failed
    6   MK_TXN_COMMIT_FAIL           PXT_OP_COMMIT failed
    7   MK_ABS_PATH_UNSUPPORTED      path started with '/' (v1.0)
    8   MK_PATH_TOO_DEEP             > 16 non-empty components
    9   MK_CAP_TAIL_FAIL             KIND_USER owner-stamp failed
    10  MK_PARENT_REF_UNSUPPORTED    a path component is exactly ".."
                                     (subtree containment guard, ENH-004)

The exit codes obey the tooling plan §4 shared convention: 0 success, 2
user error, 3 system error, 4 capability denied. Codes 3..9 map onto the
`MkdirState::MK_*` constants in `src/mkdir_state.pdx`.

# CAPABILITIES

Five caps are required at spawn, all seeded via the kernel loader's
InitCap sidecar (`_init_caps` in `src/mkdir.pdx`, validated at
load time by `init_caps_validate` — design/loader/init-caps-sidecar.md
§4 in paideia-os). Every row below is authoritative against
`caps.decl`'s `requires:` list (mkdir.ENH-008 closed a gap where
`caps.decl` was missing slot 4 even though this table, `_init_caps`,
and `manifest.pdxsig` all already listed it).

**Correction (mkdir.ENH-008):** `cap_manifest_verify` is a libpdx-cap
userspace primitive that compares a caps.decl parse against a received
`Cap[]` wire array; mkdir's `_start` does NOT call it, and no confirmed
call site anywhere in the ecosystem produces the "received" argument it
would need for a spawned coreutil (the shell's own exec path performs
its own inline cap narrowing rather than calling this function). The
actual enforcement point for the table below is the kernel loader's
InitCap validator, which checks slot range and known-kind membership at
image-load time.

    slot 0   KIND_PDXFS_TXN          R_PDXFS_TXN_INVOKE | R_PDXFS_TXN_OBSERVE
    slot 1   KIND_PDXFS_FILE         R_PDXFS_FILE_INVOKE   (target parent)
    slot 2   KIND_USER               R_USER_INVOKE         (invoker)
    slot 3   KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT       R_IPC_WRITE | R_IPC_INVOKE  (semantic pipe)
    slot 4   KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT       R_IPC_WRITE | R_IPC_INVOKE  (audit journal, M3-002)

# OUTPUT SCHEMA

    CreatedDirRecord@0.1 = {
      path_ptr        : u64,    // pointer to path string base
      path_len        : u64,    // byte length up to this component's end
      parent_txn_id   : u64,    // the TXN under which this level was created
      owner_slot_ref  : u64,    // sidecar slot of the KIND_USER cap (== 2)
    }

The stream ships one record per newly-created level (pre-existing
levels under `-p` do NOT appear in the stream). Consumers include
`ls --long` (via libpdx-cap M3-001's `KIND_USER_ref` decode helper)
and `undo` (via the paired `RmdirUndoRecord[]` in the audit journal).

# DIFFERENCES FROM POSIX

    - `-p` is atomic. POSIX mkdir(1) with -p walks the path
      non-atomically; a mid-walk failure leaves the partial prefix on
      disk. paideia-os `mkdir -p` wraps the entire walk in a single
      KIND_PDXFS_TXN and rolls the whole prefix back on any step failure.

    - Owner is always the invoker. POSIX mkdir inherits directory
      ownership from the process's effective UID. paideia-os `mkdir`
      stamps the invoker's KIND_USER cap into the inode's cap-tail
      directly — the KIND_USER_ref survives cap re-issuance and does
      not depend on a numeric UID table.

    - No -m flag. Mode bits are not a paideia-os concept; access is
      cap-mediated via KIND_PDXFS_FILE rights, not chmod bits.
      A future `mkdir --caps <caps.decl>` may take a per-directory
      cap-manifest override; not in scope for v1.0.

    - Absolute paths refused at v1.0. mkdir writes only under the
      invoker's own PdxFS subtree; a leading '/' returns
      MK_ABS_PATH_UNSUPPORTED. Cross-subtree writes wait on the
      libpdx-elevate hop landing in a post-1.0 release.

    - Parent-reference (`..`) components refused. A path component of
      exactly two bytes ".." returns MK_PARENT_REF_UNSUPPORTED before
      any cap invocation (ENH-004). This is the second half of the
      invoker-subtree guarantee above: the leading-'/' check alone does
      not stop `mkdir -p ../../etc` from walking outside the invoker's
      own subtree. A component that merely contains ".." (`..foo`,
      `foo..`) is ordinary and still succeeds.

    - No silent failure. Empty positional argv returns MK_MISSING_PATH
      with a stderr diagnostic (I4 cross-tool contract).

# EXAMPLES

    # Single level
    $ mkdir a
    $ ls -l a
    drwxr-x---  <invoker>  a/

    # Multi-level atomic
    $ mkdir -p src/kernel/core/loader
    $ echo $?
    0

    # Mixed pre-existing (a exists; b, c do not)
    $ mkdir a
    $ mkdir -p a/b/c
    $ ls a
    b/

    # Verbose
    $ mkdir -pv x/y/z
    mkdir: created x
    mkdir: created x/y
    mkdir: created x/y/z

    # Dry run
    $ mkdir -p --dry-run foo/bar
    $ ls foo
    ls: foo: no such directory

    # Undo the mkdir
    $ mkdir -p one/two/three
    $ undo
    undo: removed one, one/two, one/two/three (3 levels)

